course: geography
lesson: 9
title: The United States
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In EU5 your colonies in North America can break away from their European rulers, and that is exactly what 13 British colonies on the Atlantic coast did in 1776.
explain: But the game's "empty" colonial land was never empty: millions of Native Americans lived there, and disease, wars and forced removals took their lands and many lives.
explain: The young country grew by buying land, like the Louisiana Purchase from France and Alaska from Russia, by wars and by settlers pushing west, until it stretched from ocean to ocean.
explain: Its shape is easy to remember: old, worn Appalachians in the east, the flat Great Plains in the middle and the high, young Rockies in the west.
explain: Its people came from everywhere: Native nations, Spanish settlers in Florida and the south-west, Dutch and English colonists, enslaved Africans and millions of later immigrants.
fact: The United States has 50 states and a capital, Washington, D.C., which is not part of any state, and Alaska and Hawaii became the 49th and 50th states in 1959.
fact: The United States has about 340 million people, the third largest population after India and China, and covers about 9.8 million square kilometres.
fact: The United States began as 13 British colonies along the Atlantic coast, which declared independence on 4 July 1776 and were recognised as independent in 1783.
fact: Millions of Native Americans lived in North America before Europeans came, and diseases such as smallpox (new to them, so their bodies had no defences), wars and forced removals such as the Trail of Tears in the 1830s took their lands and many lives.
fact: Around 400,000 enslaved Africans were shipped directly to the land that became the United States, and bringing in new captives was banned in 1808, but children of enslaved mothers were enslaved too, so by 1860 about 4 million enslaved people lived there, until slavery was abolished in 1865.
fact: The Louisiana Purchase from France in 1803 roughly doubled the size of the United States, and Alaska was bought from Russia in 1867 for 7.2 million dollars.
fact: The United States has the old, worn Appalachian Mountains in the east, the flat Great Plains in the middle and the high, young Rocky Mountains in the west.
fact: Alaska is the largest state by area, though cold and remote, so few people live there, and Rhode Island the smallest, while California has the most people, about 39 million, followed by Texas.
fact: New York City is the largest city in the United States, with about 8.5 million people, and began as the Dutch town of New Amsterdam, taken by the English in 1664.
fact: Spanish settlers founded St Augustine in Florida in 1565, the oldest city founded by Europeans in the 48 neighbouring states that has been lived in ever since, and Spain and then Mexico ruled the south-west, with places such as Los Angeles and San Francisco, before the United States did; today about one in five Americans is Hispanic or Latino.
fact: Since a presidential order in 2025, English has been the official language of the United States, after more than 200 years with none, because English was used everywhere anyway while many other languages were spoken too, though no law of Congress makes it so, and many states set their own rules.
fact: Yellowstone, created in 1872, was the world's first national park, and it sits on top of a huge volcanic hotspot that feeds its geysers.
fact: D.C. stands for District of Columbia, and the capital was put outside every state so that no single state would control the national government.
exercise: How many states does the United States have? | 50 | 48 | 52 | 13
exercise: Which were the 49th and 50th states, both in 1959? | Alaska and Hawaii | Texas and California | Puerto Rico and Guam | Alaska and Florida
exercise: Is Washington, D.C. part of a state? | No, it is a separate district | Yes, it is in Virginia | Yes, it is in Washington State | Yes, it is in Maryland
exercise: Which two countries have more people than the United States? | India and China | China and Russia | India and Indonesia | Brazil and China
exercise: How many colonies first formed the United States? | 13 | 50 | 7 | 4
exercise: On which date did the colonies declare independence? | 4 July 1776 | 3 September 1783 | 1 January 1800 | 25 December 1776
exercise: What was the Trail of Tears? | The forced removal of Native peoples in the 1830s | A route taken by gold miners to California in 1849 | A railway across the Rockies | A war with Spain
exercise: About how many enslaved people lived in the United States by 1860? | About 4 million | About 400,000 | About 40,000 | About 40 million
exercise: When was slavery abolished in the United States? | 1865 | 1776 | 1808 | 1920
exercise: Which country sold the Louisiana Territory to the United States in 1803? | France | Spain | Britain | Mexico
exercise: Which country sold Alaska to the United States? | Russia | Canada | Britain | Denmark
exercise: Which mountain range is older, the Appalachians or the Rockies? | The Appalachians | The Rockies | They formed at the same time | Neither, both are volcanoes
exercise: What lies between the Appalachians and the Rockies? | The flat Great Plains | A great inland sea | The Sahara of America | A third tall mountain range
exercise: Which is the largest state by area? | Alaska | Texas, the Lone Star State | California | Montana
exercise: Which state has the most people? | California | Texas | New York | Florida
exercise: What was New York called before the English took it in 1664? | New Amsterdam | New Orange | Fort Hudson | New London
exercise: Which is the oldest continuously lived-in European-founded city in the 48 neighbouring states? | St Augustine, Florida | Boston, Massachusetts | Jamestown, Virginia | New Orleans
exercise: What made Yellowstone special in 1872? | It was the world's first national park | It was the first US state in the west | It was the first city in the west | It was the first railway station on the line to the Pacific
test: Why was the capital of the United States put outside every state? | So no single state would control the government | Because no state wanted it | Because it was a Native town | Because the British chose the place
test: In EU5 the colonial map of North America looks empty before Europeans arrive. What is wrong with that? | Millions of Native Americans already lived there | Nothing, the land was empty | Only a few hundred people lived there, mostly hunters by the coast | The land was all ice then
test: What made diseases so deadly for Native Americans after Europeans arrived? | They had never met these germs, so their bodies had no defences | Native doctors refused to help | They lived in cold places | Their food made the germs stronger and spread them faster from town to town
test: Why did the number of enslaved people grow even after new captives could no longer be imported in 1808? | Children born to enslaved mothers were enslaved too | More ships brought them anyway, legally | Free people sold themselves | The law counted them twice
test: Why do many US places, such as Los Angeles and San Francisco, have Spanish names? | Spain and then Mexico ruled those lands first | Spanish was the language of the 13 colonies | The names were chosen in 1959 | They were named by Columbus
test: bro says alaska is huge so loads of ppl must live there. right? | No, it is cold and remote, so few people live there | Yes, more than live in California and Texas together | Yes, it has the biggest city | No, nobody at all lives there
test: Which is true about California? | It has more people than any other state | It is the smallest state in the whole country | It joined in 1959 | It was bought from Russia
test: What heats the water that shoots out of Yellowstone's geysers? | Hot rock above a volcanic hotspot | Hot springs that flow in from the sea | The sunshine on the lakes | Burning coal under the park
test: Why did the United States go so long without an official language? | English was used everywhere anyway, and many languages were spoken | Congress could not agree on spelling | The Spanish king forbade it | English was banned after 1776
test: When the Louisiana Purchase was signed in 1803, who was left out of the deal? | The Native peoples who lived on that land | France | The United States | The Spanish settlers who had lived in New Orleans for years
test: Settlers crossing west in wagons met which mountains as the hardest barrier? | The high, young Rocky Mountains | The low Appalachians | The Great Plains | The hills of Rhode Island
exam: Ascertain the number of constituent states of the United States of America. | Fifty | Forty-eight | Fifty-two | Thirteen
exam: Identify the state from which the Territory of Alaska was acquired in 1867. | The Russian Empire | The United Kingdom | The Kingdom of Denmark | The Kingdom of Spain
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relative ages of the principal mountain ranges of the United States? | The Appalachians are older and more eroded than the Rockies | The Rockies are older and more eroded than the Appalachians | Both ranges are of identical age | Both ranges were formed in the twentieth century
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the official language of the United States. | English was so designated by presidential order in 2025 | English was so designated by Congress in 1776 | Spanish was so designated in 1565 | No language has ever been so designated
